  "primer": [
    {
      "h": "Base weight is the number people mean",
      "p": [
        "Base weight is everything in your pack <strong>minus food, water, and fuel</strong> — the stuff that doesn’t shrink as you hike. It’s the only figure that lets two kits be compared honestly.",
        "Under 20 lb is comfortable. Under 15 is light. Under 10 takes real money or real discipline."
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "A 20-degree bag is not comfortable at 20°",
      "p": [
        "Most bags are labeled with their <strong>lower limit</strong> — the point a warm sleeper survives, not sleeps. The <strong>comfort rating</strong> is usually 8–10° warmer.",
        "If you want to actually sleep well at 30°F, shop 20°F-rated. Read the ISO comfort number, not the name on the stuff sack."
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Your pad matters as much as your bag",
      "p": [
        "Cold comes up from the ground, and down crushes flat underneath you where it can’t insulate. A warm bag on a summer pad still sleeps cold.",
        "For alpine nights near freezing you want <strong>R-value 4.5 or better</strong>. Below about R 2.5 is a summer-only pad, whatever the bag says."
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "The big three own the budget",
      "p": [
        "Shelter, pack, and sleep system are <strong>half to two-thirds of your pack weight and about half your budget</strong> — check the Big Three figures on each tab above. Everything else is rounding.",
        "Spend here, in this order: sleep system first (it’s the safety item and fit matters most), then shelter, then pack — buy the pack last, once you know what volume you actually need.",
        "<strong>Get the pack fitted before you buy it.</strong> Packs are sized by torso length, not by height, and a 60 L that doesn’t fit your back carries worse than a 45 L that does. REI will measure your torso and load a pack with sandbags to walk around in, free and with no purchase. Do that before ordering anything — it’s the one item here you shouldn’t buy sight-unseen."
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Don’t buy it all at once",
      "p": [
        "Rent the tent. Borrow the pack. Buy the sleep system. Go out for one night close to home and find out what you actually hate before you commit.",
        "Wait for the sales — <strong>20% off one full-price item runs a few times a year</strong> and it’s worth timing your most expensive purchase around it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Food: about 1.5 to 2 lb per day",
      "p": [
        "Aim for <strong>120+ calories per ounce</strong> so you’re carrying energy, not packaging. Roughly 2,500–3,500 kcal a day on big-elevation days.",
        "Weigh and bag every day separately at home. It’s the only way to know what you’re carrying, and it stops you from hauling four days of food on a three-day trip."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "regulations": {
    "title": "What the wilderness requires",
    "intro": "Two of these change what you pack. The campfire rule is why there is a stove and a fuel canister on every list here and no fire kit on any of them.",
    "points": [
      "<strong>Permit:</strong> free self-issue registration form for overnight trips, available at wilderness trailheads. Nothing to reserve, nothing to pay.",
      "<strong>Group limit 15.</strong> Not a constraint for a party of three.",
      "<strong>No campfires</strong> above 11,000 ft, within ¼ mile of a lake, or within 100 ft of a stream or trail. Every camp on this route is lakeside, so treat it as stove-only and carry the fuel.",
      "<strong>Camp 100 ft from water and trails.</strong> Worth knowing before you commit to a pad footprint — the flat ground right at the shoreline is off limits.",
      "<strong>Dogs on a leash under six feet.</strong>"
    ],
    "outro": "Separately from the permanent rules above: Stage 2 fire restrictions were in force forest-wide as of <strong>July 29, 2026</strong>, with Very High danger on the Dillon district. That is a current status, not a signed order covering late August — re-check before you go. The campfire prohibition near lakes is permanent and applies either way."
